Passkey automation assessment – summary and closure

1. Recommended automation approach
- Playwright (Java) + Cucumber with Playwright's built-in WebAuthn virtual authenticator (BrowserContext.credentials, Playwright 1.61+).
- The virtual authenticator replaces the device prompt (Windows Hello / Touch ID / fingerprint), so passkey registration and sign-in run without a physical device, BrowserStack device or OS settings.
- Runs on Chromium browsers (Chrome, Edge) in English and French.
- Proof of concept passing end to end: Connect ID + password + OTP -> Register passkey -> logout -> sign in with the stored passkey -> dashboard.
- Candidate scenarios for automation: SC-001 to SC-008 (see earlier comment).

2. Resolution of the constraints identified in this story (10/Nov comment)
- MacBook on BrowserStack (system settings cannot be changed): not needed for automation, as the virtual authenticator does not depend on OS settings. MacBook/Safari is covered by manual testing on a physical device.
- BrowserStack Android (passkey registered but not visible in password manager): BrowserStack is not used for passkey automation. Android is covered by manual testing on a physical device.
- Physical devices (screen went blank when saving passkey): automation no longer depends on physical devices, since the virtual authenticator handles passkey creation and sign-in. Physical devices are used for manual testing only.
- Windows Hello: automation does not depend on Windows Hello; the virtual authenticator replaces it. Windows Hello behaviour is covered by manual testing.

3. Dependencies and limitations
- Environment: passkey sign-in failed because the RP ID was configured as "localhost" while UAP runs on authentication6.sys.td.com. After it was changed to the sys.td.com domain, passkey sign-in works. The virtual authenticator does not enforce RP ID rules, so environment config must still be verified on real devices.
- Browser UI: the passkey list under the username field (conditional UI) cannot be clicked by automation; a test-only script lets the virtual authenticator answer UAP's request.
- Test data: a customer can register a passkey only once (Register passkey pop-up is not shown again and delete passkey is not built yet). Automated runs need fresh customers or a backend passkey reset before they can run in the pipeline.
- OTP: the test environment accepts the default OTP 123456, so OTP entry is automated with no external dependency.

4. Scope split
Automation: SC-001 to SC-008 on Chrome and Edge, EN/FR.
Manual: QR code / cross-device sign-in, synced passkeys (iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager, Microsoft Password Manager), real OS prompts and passkey name display, browser passkey dropdown, Safari, iPhone/iPad, Android phone/tablet.

Approach and scenarios shared with the QE team. Closing this story as all acceptance criteria are met.
